# The three-valued X <= Y of balls with one-limb centers (milestone2.md, "One enclosure interface",
# "Comparisons"): yes when every member of X is at most every member of Y, r_x + r_y <= c_y - c_x,
# by le_2_2 on the exact pairs and a test that the sum of the radii is finite; no when every member
# of X is above every member of Y, r_x + r_y < c_x - c_y, by lt_2_2. Both false for an empty operand
# and for the whole line. Exact when r_x + r_y <= Omega (and also, not claimed, when the difference
# of the centers does not overflow).
kernel:        ball_less_equal_1
input:         stored cx
input:         stored rx
input:         stored cy
input:         stored ry
output:        mask yes
output:        mask no
requires:      ball cx rx
requires:      ball cy ry
ensures:       trit yes no le x y x=cx,rx y=cy,ry exact_when=abs_le(rx+ry,omega)
min_precision: 2
source:        the sum of the radii and the differences of the centers as exact pairs, compared (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        unverified
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   unverified
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the results are masks, which have no non-finite value, and a comparison with NaN is false; trit states what comes out for every canonical ball

(s0, s1) = two_sum_ord(rx, ry)
ncx      = neg(cx)
(d0, d1) = two_sum_ord(cy, ncx)
ncy      = neg(cy)
(g0, g1) = two_sum_ord(cx, ncy)
a        = le_2_2(s0, s1, d0, d1)
sa       = abs(s0)
fs       = lt(sa, const inf)
yes      = and(a, fs)
no       = lt_2_2(s0, s1, g0, g1)
